"Private-RAG: Answering Multiple Queries with LLMs while Keeping Your Data Private (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.07637v1)\nciteturn2011view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1993view1\",\"lineno\":59}); Total lines: 572\nL56: In domains like healthcare or law, the retrieved content may expose private records, raising serious privacy concerns. Prior work has shown that RAG systems without proper safeguards are vulnerable to information leakage (cite35†Naseh et al., 2025 ; cite36†Liu et al., 2025 ; cite37†Anderson et al., 2024 ; cite38†Li et al., 2025 ; cite39†Zhang et al., 2025 ; cite40†Zeng et al., 2024a ; cite41†Jiang et al., 2024 ; cite42†Peng et al., 2024 ), compromising data owner privacy and user trust.\nL57: Differential privacy (DP) is a widely adopted framework for providing rigorous guarantees on individual data protection. Recent work (cite43†Koga et al., 2024 ) has proposed DPSparseVoteRAG, a RAG system that ensures the generated answer satisfies DP with respect to the external dataset, for a single user query. Empirical results demonstrate that this approach outperforms the baseline using a public LLM without the external dataset, while achieving an $\\varepsilon$-DP guarantee with $\\varepsilon\\approx 10$.\nL58: In realistic deployments, many queries may be issued by one or more users. A naïve approach that applies DPSparseVoteRAG to each query and relies on standard composition theorems quickly exhausts a reasonable privacy budget. As our experimental results (Figure cite44†2 ) show, to achieve reasonable utility, this approach may require a privacy budget as large as $\\varepsilon=1000$, which is generally considered too weak. This raises a key question:\nL59: Can we design a differentially private RAG algorithm that handles hundreds of queries while ensuring both meaningful privacy and utility?\nL60: \nL61: We answer this question affirmatively and summarize our contributions below.\nL62: Circumventing Query-Composition Overhead with Per-Document Rényi Filters. We propose a novel framework for multi-query differentially private RAG. Rather than composing a sequence of single-query DP-RAG executions, where the privacy budget grows with the number of queries, we leverage individual R’enyi filters (cite45†Feldman & Zrnic, 2021 ). These filters bound privacy loss based on how many times each document is retrieved, yielding substantial savings when queries access largely disjoint documents.\nL63: To the best of our knowledge, this is the first application of privacy filters in the RAG setting. Our framework can incorporate any single-query private RAG algorithm.\nL64: Two DP Multi-RAG Algorithms for Varying Test Query Dependencies. We propose two differentially private RAG algorithms for the multi-query setting through threshold-based screening of relevant documents and their are tailored to the degree of relevance among test-time queries. MuRAG (Algorithm cite46†1 ) uses a fixed relevance threshold across all queries and is sufficient to work well for settings where queries are independent and do not share relevant private documents.\nL65: MuRAG-Ada (Algorithm cite47†2 ) allocates a small portion of the privacy budget to release a query-specific relevance threshold, enabling more efficient use of the budget when queries are related and share overlapping relevant documents.\nL66: Practical Multi-Query RAG with Non-Trivial Privacy Guarantees. We evaluate our algorithms through extensive experiments on three LLMs (OPT-1.3B, Pythia-1.4B, and Mistral-7B). Our evaluation spans three types of datasets: standard RAG benchmarks (Natural Questions, Trivia Questions), a more challenging multi-hop QA dataset (MQuAKE) with correlated questions, and a privacy-sensitive application (ChatDoctor) consisting of patient–doctor QA pairs.\nL67: Empirical results show that both of our methods can answer hundreds of queries within a total privacy budget of $\\varepsilon\\approx 10$ while maintaining reasonable utility, a trade-off no baseline method achieves. Furthermore, we demonstrate that our approaches with $\\varepsilon=10$ effectively defend against a state-of-the-art multi-query membership inference attack for RAG.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPrivate-RAG: Answering Multiple Queries with LLMs while Keeping Your Data Private (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.07637v1)\nciteturn2011view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1993view1\",\"lineno\":180}); Total lines: 572\nL172: Notice that in Algorithm cite47†2 , we use $k$ as a stopping criterion instead of releasing differentially private top-$k$ relevance scores. This is because releasing a noisy top-$k$ score for each query would make the privacy budget grow linearly with the number of queries and incur loss on all documents, thereby breaking the per-document privacy filter.\nL173: By contrast, our prefix-sum approach (Step 1 of Algorithm cite47†2 ) incurs privacy loss only on the documents that appear in the released prefix sums, while all other documents remain untouched. This concentrates the privacy cost of this step still on a small subset, yielding tighter accounting and more efficient budget use across multiple queries. The privacy guarantee of MuRAG-Ada is stated in Theorem cite53†2 , and the proof is deferred to Appendix cite52†D .\nL174: ###### Theorem 2 (Privacy Guarantee of Algorithm cite47†2 ).\nL175: \nL176: MuRAG-Ada satisfies $\\varepsilon$-differential privacy provided that the initial privacy budget allocated to each document $z\\in D$ is at most $\\varepsilon$.\nL177: \nL178: ## 4 Experiment\nL179: \nL180: Figure 1: Histogram of document reuse across questions. Each bar shows how many questions a document appears in among the top-$K$ retrieved results ($K=50$). The x-axis indicates the number of questions per document, and the y-axis shows the count of such documents.\nL181: ### 4.1 Dataset\nL182: Datasets set-up. We first evaluate our methods on two independent question sets: Natural Questions and Trivia Questions. These are standard benchmarks for evaluating RAG systems and have been used in prior work on per-query DP for RAG (cite43†Koga et al., 2024 ). Following their setup, we randomly subsample 100 questions from each dataset to reduce computational overhead.\nL183: Importantly, the questions are independent of one another, and each requires a disjoint set of relevant documents from the external database. To quantify document reuse, we examine how frequently each document appears in the top-$K$ retrieved results ($K=50$) across questions. As shown in Figure cite54†1 , in both Natural Questions and Trivia Questions, most documents are retrieved for only one or two queries. Thus, we expect MuRAG to perform sufficiently well on these two datasets.\nL184: Second, we consider a correlated question set, MQuAKE (cite55†Zhong et al., ). This dataset contains sequences of semantically related single-hop questions that together form multi-hop reasoning chains. We select 100 such sequences, yielding 400 individual questions for evaluation. Since questions in the same sequence share entities (subjects or objects), their relevant documents substantially overlap. As shown in Figure cite54†1 , many documents appear across multiple questions.\nL185: We therefore expect MuRAG-Ada to have an advantage over MuRAG.\nL186: Finally, we evaluate on ChatDoctor (cite56†Li et al., 2023 ), a privacy-sensitive application of RAG in the healthcare domain. This dataset consists of QA interactions between patients and doctors. We sample 100 patient questions as our test set. This evaluation tests the effectiveness of our methods in a real-world sensitive setting and their robustness against privacy attacks.\nL187: External datasets reflecting both standard and privacy-sensitive settings. For Natural Questions, Trivia Questions, and MQuAKE Questions, we use Wikipedia of $\\sim 20M$ documents as the external knowledge source following the standard RAG setup (cite57†Chen et al., 2017 ; cite58†Lewis et al., 2020 ). For ChatDoctor Questions, the external dataset consists of the remaining $\\sim 200K$ QA pairs from the original ChatDoctor dataset, excluding the 100 patient questions used for testing.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPrivate-RAG: Answering Multiple Queries with LLMs while Keeping Your Data Private (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.07637v1)\nciteturn2011view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1993view1\",\"lineno\":436}); Total lines: 572\nL401: Algorithm 7 DP-RAG$(x,D,\\mathrm{LLM},\\varepsilon)$\nL402: \nL403: Input: Prompt $x$; document collection $D$; language model $\\mathrm{LLM}$; total budget $\\varepsilon$.\nL404: \nL405: Require: Per-token budget $\\varepsilon_{0}$; max tokens $T_{\\max}$; voters $m$; docs per voter $k$; retriever $R$; vote threshold $\\theta$.\nL406: \nL407: Set: $\\varepsilon_{\\text{Lap}}\\leftarrow\\varepsilon_{\\text{Expo}}\\leftarrow\\varepsilon_{0}/2$; discoveries left $c\\leftarrow\\lfloor\\varepsilon/\\varepsilon_{0}\\rfloor$\nL408: 1 $\\hat{\\theta}\\leftarrow\\theta+\\mathrm{Lap}(2/\\varepsilon_{\\text{Lap}})$ $\\triangleright$ noisy threshold\nL409: \nL410: 2 $D_{x}\\leftarrow R(x,D;mk)$; split $D_{x}$ uniformly into $m$ chunks $\\{D_{x}^{(i)}\\}_{i=1}^{m}$.\nL411: \nL412: 3 for $t\\leftarrow 1$ to $T_{\\max}$ do\nL413: \nL414:     4 $b\\leftarrow\\mathrm{LLM}(x,\\emptyset\\mid y_{<t})$ $\\triangleright$ baseline token (no RAG)\nL415: \nL416:     5 for $i\\leftarrow 1$ to $m$ do\nL417: \nL418:        6 $v_{i}\\leftarrow\\mathrm{LLM}(x,D_{x}^{(i)}\\mid y_{<t})$\nL419:     7 $\\vec{u}\\leftarrow\\textsc{Count}(\\{v_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{m},\\mathcal{V})$;  $s\\leftarrow H[b]$ $\\triangleright$ Algorithm cite108†5 L420: \nL421:     8 if $s+\\mathrm{Lap}(4/\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{Lap}})\\leq\\hat{\\theta}$ then\nL422: \nL423:        9 $y_{t}\\leftarrow\\textsc{ExpoMech}(\\vec{u},\\mathcal{V},\\varepsilon_{\\text{Expo}})$ $\\triangleright$ Algorithm cite109†6 L424: \nL425:        10 $c\\leftarrow c-1$\nL426: \nL427:        11 $\\hat{\\theta}\\leftarrow\\theta+\\mathrm{Lap}(2/\\varepsilon_{\\text{Lap}})$\nL428: \nL429:     12 else\nL430:        13 $y_{t}\\leftarrow b$ $\\triangleright$ keep baseline\nL431: \nL432:     14 if $y_{t}=\\langle\\mathtt{EOS}\\rangle$ or $c=0$ then\nL433: \nL434:        15 return $(y_{1},\\ldots,y_{t})$\nL435: \nL436: 16 return $(y_{1},\\ldots,y_{T_{\\max}})$\nL437: \nL438: We now give the privacy guarantee for Algorithm cite50†7 .\nL439: ###### Lemma 1 (Privacy Guarantee for Algorithm cite50†7 ).\nL440: \nL441: Algorithm cite50†7 satisfies $\\varepsilon$-DP under add/remove relationship.\nL442: ###### Proof.\nL443: \nL444: Notice that Algorithm cite50†7 is an instantiation of AboveThreshold (cite111†Dwork et al. (2014, Algorithm 1) ) with at most $c$ discoveries. It therefore suffices to show that each discovery event (i.e., each use of the exponential mechanism) satisfies $\\varepsilon_{0}$-DP, where $c=\\lfloor\\varepsilon/\\varepsilon_{0}\\rfloor$.\nL445: We first verify that the added noise meets the requirements of the stated privacy guarantee, namely for the threshold perturbation (Line 8 of Algorithm cite50†7 ) and for the exponential mechanism (Line 9 of Algorithm cite50†7 ). Without loss of generality, assume the input document set has size larger than $mk$. Consider two neighboring datasets $D$ and $D^{\\prime}$ such that $\\lvert D\\setminus D^{\\prime}\\rvert+\\lvert D^{\\prime}\\setminus D\\rvert\\leq 1$.\nL446: This implies $\\lvert D_{x}\\setminus D_{x}^{\\prime}\\rvert+\\lvert D_{x}^{\\prime}\\setminus D_{x}\\rvert\\leq 2$, since the retriever $R$ ranks documents by relevance and selects the top-$mk$ entries. Replacing a single token in the voting results can change at most one bin count in the histogram by $1$, so the score function satisfies\nL447:  | $$\\lvert s(D)-s(D^{\\prime})\\rvert\\;=\\;\\lvert s(D_{x})-s(D_{x}^{\\prime})\\rvert\\;\\leq\\;1,$$  |\nL448: \nL449: where $s$ is defined in Line 7 of Algorithm cite50†7 . Similarly, the utility function has unit sensitivity for each token, i.e.,\nL450: \nL451:  | $$\\lvert u_{j}(D_{x})-u_{j}(D_{x}^{\\prime})\\rvert\\leq 1,\\quad\\forall j\\in\\{1,\\ldots,|\\mathcal{V}|\\},$$  |\nL452: \nL453: where $u_{j}$ is the $j$-th coordinate of $\\vec{u}$.\nL454: Thus, by cite111†Dwork et al. (2014, Theorem 3.23) and adaptive composition, each discovery is $\\varepsilon_{0}$-DP. Since the number of discoveries satisfies $c=\\lfloor\\varepsilon/\\varepsilon_{0}\\rfloor$, basic composition implies that the entire execution of Algorithm cite50†7 satisfies $\\varepsilon$-DP. ∎\nL455: ### C.3 Baseline Algorithms for Differentially Private Multi-Query RAG\nL456: \nL457: (Naive-Multi-RAG) Algorithm cite64†8 defines a naïve baseline for DP multi-query RAG: it answers each query $q_{t}$ independently by invoking the single-query DP-RAG procedure (Algorithm cite50†7 ) on the private dataset $D$ with per-query budget $\\varepsilon_{q}$, yielding responses $\\{a_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{T}$.\nL458: \nL459: Algorithm 8 Naive-Multi-RAG\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThink Before You Retrieve: Learning Test-Time Adaptive Search with Small Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.07581v1)\nciteturn2011view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1993view2\",\"lineno\":245}); Total lines: 1032\nL233: BM25 (dense)  | 82.5  | 70.0  | 64.5  | 37.0\nL241: ### 4.3 Results\nL242: Classic information retrieval benchmarks. On BEIR tasks (Table cite138†2 , nDCG@10), we observe distinct performance patterns across fact-checking scenarios. For scientific verification like SciFact, our approach performs competitively with baselines, while on FEVER we achieve comparable results to LLMs but trail specialized retrievers like DeepRetrieval (84.1%) and BM25 dense (82.5%).\nL243: This divide suggests that learned search strategies show benefits when domain expertise is required, though lexical matching remains effective. Our models also show consistent performance on biomedical tasks like NFCorpus where traditional dense methods struggle.\nL244: Reasoning-intensive retrieval tasks. BRIGHT (Table cite139†3 ) shows varied performance patterns across reasoning domains. Our overall average compares favorably to baselines, with notable performance on coding tasks where we achieve 32.9% on Pony while trailing BM25 dense on LeetCode. We observe consistent results across the theorem-based category, with Orion-Medium achieving the highest score on AoPS, despite no math or coding training data.\nL245: These results suggest that learned search strategies transfer across complex reasoning scenarios.\nL246: ## 5 Discussion\nL247: ##### Does RL help beyond SFT?\nL248: Table 3: nDCG@10 scores (%) on reasoning-intensive retrieval tasks from the BRIGHT benchmark: biology (Bio.), earth science (Earth.), economics (Econ.), psychology (Psy.), robotics (Rob.), stack overflow (Stack.), sustainable living (Sus.), LeetCode (Leet.), Pony, AoPS, TheoremQA with question retrieval (TheoQ.) and with theorem retrieval (TheoT.). “Avg.” denotes the macro average score across 12 datasets. Standard deviations are omitted as they are negligible (often zero).\nL249: The best score on each dataset is shown in bold. ^{∗}Parameter counts approximated by cite137†Abacha et al. 1 .\nL250:  | StackExchange  | Coding  | Theorem-based  |\nL251: Model/Retriever  | Bio.  | Earth.  | Econ.  | Psy.  | Rob.  | Stack.  | Sus.  | Leet.  | Pony  | AoPS  | TheoQ.  | TheoT.  | Avg.\nL252: General-Purpose LLMs\nL253: GPT-4.1  | 39.7  | 