f_id\":\"turn1996view1\",\"lineno\":450}); Total lines: 1888\nL439@P10: We varied problem complexity by the number of n units per group and the number k units required for\nL440@P10: surrounding. Figure 3 reveals two insights. First, Human-TN increases the task success rate regardless\nL441@P10: of the base capability of the LLM used in the system; at (n = 10, k = 6), the 20b model Human-TN\nL442@P10: outperforms 120b No-TN. Second, higher (n, k) values seem show a non-linear correlation with task\nL443@P10: difficulty; future work should explore whether the LLM is exploiting problem structure to solve larger\nL444@P10: problems.\nL445@P10: 6 Discussion\nL446@P10: The evaluation of ProcLLM shows that Human-TN and LLM-TN improve task success rate, often significantly\nL447@P10: and even across No-TN success capability and different benchmarks. We also varied problem complexity\nL448@P10: and showed that, in BW and UM, the LLM system with a task planner seems to have a slower decrease\nL449@P10-11: 11in task success rate than the LLM system without a task planner. This is true in both domains for\nL450@P11: GPT-oss-120b, but the results for GPT-oss-20b show less improvement; we hypothesize that smaller\nL451@P11: models such as GPT-oss-20b can run into context length issues that are not addressed by our task\nL452@P11: network approach. We further examine several more questions about these results.\nL453@P11: Why is No-TN doing so poorly? Although not obvious from the results presented so far, there are some\nL454@P11: subtle differences in model behavior that we would like to discuss. To explain, consider the first few\nL455@P11: steps that an LLM may need to take to solve one of the agentic problems seen so far. In each of the\nL456@P11: problems, there is a request.txt and a problem specification.txt. The first few steps of solving an\nL457@P11: agentic task is to read each of these files and then take notes on the files into the short-term memory file\nL458@P11: (Notes). Some models (e.g., nemotron, codestral, llama) are naturally proficient in this and will generate\nL459@P11: a sequence of actions:\nL460@P11: 1. Read request.txt\nL461@P11: 2. Append [request details]\nL462@P11: 3. Read problem specification.txt\nL463@P11: 4. Append [spec details]\nL464@P11: This sequence of actions can be generated even if the task network does not have explicit task nodes\nL465@P11: about taking notes (or even without a task network at all). However, our experiments with GPT-oss-120b\nL466@P11: revealed the system would get stuck in loops like:\nL467@P11: 1. Read request.txt\nL468@P11: 2. Read problem specification.txt\nL469@P11: 3. Read request.txt\nL470@P11: 4. Read problem specification.txt\nL471@P11: 5. etc.\nL472@P11: This causes the system to timeout and fail as a result. We can alleviate this problem for domains such\nL473@P11: as BW by explicitly extending the final goal for the task. For example, we could change solve the user\nL474@P11: request into solve the user request and have notes on the request and problem specification. But\nL475@P11: such\nL476@P11: changes may not scale if a problem has many goals.\nL477@P11: Are LLM-constructed task networks better? (LLM-TN) To study LLM-TN, we constructed a prompt\nL478@P11: that includes the problem specification, the agent prompt, and two valid task network node examples\nL479@P11: and asked Gemini 2.5 Pro to produce a task network (LLM-TN). In Figure 3, we see that task success\nL480@P11: rate of LLM-TN (brown bars) does better than No-TN while varying against Human-TN.\nL481@P11: Does Human-TN or LLM-TN save runtime? Figure 4 shows runtime statistics for GPT-oss-120b in\nL482@P11: ProcLLM across the evaluation benchmarks (Figure A.2 shows full details). For Blocks World and Unit\nL483@P11: Movement, the statistics are averaged over all problem sizes.\nL484@P11: In three of the four benchmarks, the task planner decreases the number of system iterations required\nL485@P11: for a given problem instance. The exception is Recipe Generator, where the LLM with No-TN produces\nL486@P11: quick but always incorrect outputs, unlike other benchmarks where No-TN typically fails via timeout.\nL487@P11: One explanation is that the LLM can treat the request in Recipe Generator as a query to be answered\n"
